[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Variable Scopes in Postman"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variables are essential in Postman for storing and reusing values across your requests and scripts. Postman organizes variables into different scopes, determining their availability and lifespan. Choosing the correct scope ensures security, maintainability, and efficiency in your API testing workflows."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The 5 Variable Scopes in Postman"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Global Variables: Available across all workspaces and collections. Best for generic data like base URLs."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Collection Variables: Available for all requests within a specific collection. Ideal for collection-specific configuration."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Environment Variables: Tied to active environments (e.g., Development, Staging, Production). Perfect for environment-specific tokens."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Variables: Sourced from external CSV or JSON files during collection runs using the Collection Runner."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Local Variables: Temporary variables scoped to a single request execution. Cleared immediately after the request finishes."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting and Using Variables via Scripts"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can programmatically set and retrieve variables inside Postman's Pre-request and Test scripts using JavaScript syntax. Below is an example demonstrating different scopes."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Setting an Environment variable
pm.environment.set("env_token", "abc123xyz");

// Setting a Collection variable
pm.collectionVariables.set("collection_id", "98765");

// Setting a Global variable
pm.globals.set("global_timeout", 5000);

// Setting a Local variable (temporary for this request)
pm.variables.set("temp_hash", "sha256_hash_value");

// Retrieving variables
console.log(pm.variables.get("env_token"));"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Variable Precedence and Resolution"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you reference a variable using double curly braces (e.g., {{variable_name}}), Postman resolves it based on scope priority. If a variable exists in multiple scopes, Postman uses the following order from highest to lowest: Local -> Data -> Environment -> Collection -> Global."},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices for API Testing"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never hardcode sensitive credentials like API keys or passwords; store them in Environment variables and mark them as type 'secret'."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Collection variables for endpoints and parameters that remain constant across a specific API workflow."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Clean up Global variables frequently to prevent pollution across different workspaces."}]
